  "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}=2{x}^{2}(3-2x).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}(3-2x)=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(-1) & =2(-1)^{2}(3-2(-1))=10>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(1) & =2(1)^{2}(3-2(1))=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(2) & =2(2)^{2}(3-2(2))=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{\u2032}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\u2197$ |  | $\u2197$ | max | $\u2198$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
